{"qas": [
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2305.14865, what are two reasons why Stackelberg games are more challenging to compute than Nash equilibria in general-sum settings?", "answer": "Stackelberg and Nash equilibria typically diverge in general-sum games, and structural properties that enable efficient computation for Nash equilibrium are insufficient for Stackelberg equilibrium.", "evidence": [{"page": 4, "quote": "In zero-sum games, [13] shows that Stackelberg and Nash equilibria are interchangeable, but the two typically diverge under the general-sum setting. Moreover, structural properties that lead to efficient computation of Nash equilibrium is not sufficient for the case of Stackelberg equilibrium, which shows the latter is more challenging to compute compared to the former [13]."}, {"page": 8, "quote": "As highlighted earlier, Stackelberg and Nash equilibria typically diverge in the general-sum scenarios."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2305.14865, what are two main factors that justify assigning the leader role to AI corporations in civil domains?", "answer": "AI innovation benefits from firms' exploration, and governance ministries struggle to foresee AI risks, making firms better suited as leaders in civil domains.", "evidence": [{"page": 11, "quote": "From the viewpoint to maintain continuous creativity in AI, it is critical to provide enough space for firms in this area to allow for exploration and experiments."}, {"page": 11, "quote": "a well-known fact is that it is very challenging for the governance ministry to foresee AI development direction and possible risks, which are essential to formulate optimal guidance thereof."}]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2305.14865, what does the abbreviation 'SE' stand for, and how is it defined?", "answer": "'SE' stands for Stackelberg Equilibrium, defined as a strategy profile where the leader's strategy is optimal given the follower's best response under optimistic tie-breaking.", "evidence": [{"page": 8, "quote": "Definition 2.2 (Stackelberg Equilibrium (SE)). The strategy θ∗ is a Stackelberg solution for the leader if supω∈ω∗(θ∗ )J(θ∗ , ω) ≤ supω∈ω∗(θ)J(θ, ω), ∀θ ∈ Θ; if ω∗ is the follower's best response strategy under optimistic tie-breaking defined in Eq. 2, then the strategy profile (θ∗ , ω∗ ) is a Stackelberg equilibrium on Θ × W."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2305.14865, what are the six dimensions used to evaluate AI technologies in the Stackelberg framework for AI governance?", "answer": "The six dimensions are performance, robustness, explainability, fairness, privacy, and security.", "evidence": [{"page": 10, "quote": "In this framework, players evaluate the AI technologies from six dimension aspects: performance, robustness, explainability, fairness, privacy, and security."}]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2305.14865, what is the main challenge for public policy makers as AI becomes more complex?", "answer": "Public policy makers may struggle to keep up with state-of-the-art AI methods as the field grows more complicated.", "evidence": [{"page": 6, "quote": "As AI becomes more complicated, public policy makers might struggle to catch up with state-of-the-art methods at the AI frontier."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2305.14865, what is the key property of incentive games that benefits the leader?", "answer": "Incentive games allow the leader to improve their total utility by offering incentives that benefit the follower.", "evidence": [{"page": 14, "quote": "A primary property of incentive games is that the additional incentives that only benefit the follower can actually improve the leader's total utility in equilibrium."}]}
]}
